 # nano-pdf
 
 Edit PDFs using natural-language instructions. Point it at a page and describe what to change.
 
+## When to Use
+
+- User wants to change text in a PDF without opening it in a PDF editor
+- Fix a typo, update a date, rename a client, or change a title in a PDF
+- User says "edit this PDF", "fix the typo on page 2", or "update the contract"
+- **Not for**: structural layout changes, adding/removing pages, form-filling, or image editing — use a dedicated PDF editor for those
+
 ## Prerequisites
 
 ```bash
@@ -22,6 +32,14 @@ uv pip install nano-pdf
 
 # Or with pip
 pip install nano-pdf
+
+# Verify installation
+nano-pdf --version
+```
+
+nano-pdf requires an LLM API key. Check which one it uses:
+```bash
+nano-pdf --help | grep -i "key\|api\|model"
 ```
 
 ## Usage
@@ -30,6 +48,8 @@ pip install nano-pdf
 nano-pdf edit <file.pdf> <page_number> "<instruction>"
 ```
 
+The command **modifies the file in place**. Always back up first if the original matters.
+
 ## Examples
 
 ```bash
@@ -41,11 +61,33 @@ nano-pdf edit report.pdf 3 "Update the date from January to February 2026"
 
 # Fix content
 nano-pdf edit contract.pdf 2 "Change the client name from 'Acme Corp' to 'Acme Industries'"
+
+# Fix typo
+nano-pdf edit memo.pdf 1 "Fix the spelling of 'recieve' to 'receive' throughout the page"
+```
+
+## Workflow: Safe Edit Pattern
+
+```bash
+# 1. Back up the original
+cp report.pdf report.pdf.bak
+
+# 2. Apply the edit
+nano-pdf edit report.pdf 2 "Change the date to April 11, 2026"
+
+# 3. Verify — check file size changed (crude but quick)
+ls -lh report.pdf report.pdf.bak
+
+# 4. Tell the user to open it for visual verification
+echo "Edit applied. Please open report.pdf to verify the change looks correct."
 ```
 
-## Notes
+## Pitfalls
 
-- Page numbers may be 0-based or 1-based depending on version — if the edit hits the wrong page, retry with ±1
-- Always verify the output PDF after editing (use `read_file` to check file size, or open it)
-- The tool uses an LLM under the hood — requires an API key (check `nano-pdf --help` for config)
-- Works well for text changes; complex layout modifications may need a different approach
+- **Page indexing ambiguity**: nano-pdf page numbers may be 0-based or 1-based depending on version. If the edit hits the wrong page, retry with `±1`. Always confirm page numbering with the user if uncertain.
+- **In-place modification**: nano-pdf edits the file in place — there is no `--output` flag in most versions. Backup the file before editing (`cp file.pdf file.pdf.bak`).
+- **LLM API key required**: nano-pdf calls an LLM to interpret your instruction. If no API key is configured, it will fail silently or error. Check `nano-pdf --help` for the key/model config.
+- **Complex layouts fail**: Text in tables, headers/footers, text boxes, or multi-column layouts may not be editable — the tool works best on simple body text.
+- **Scanned PDFs are image-only**: If the PDF is a scanned image (no embedded text), nano-pdf cannot edit it. Use the `ocr-and-documents` skill first to extract text, then recreate the PDF.
+- **Instruction scope**: Instructions are per-page. To change text on multiple pages, run separate commands for each page.
+- **File corruption risk**: Malformed PDF structures may cause silent corruption. Always verify the output file is readable after editing.
